note that the sum of angles within a  triangle is 180 degrees let's prove that  for triangle ABC to prove that we need  to do a small bit of construction we  draw a line EF passing through point a  and parallel to BC it will look like  this it passes through point a and is  parallel to BC you can see that many  angles are formed let's name them  let this be angle one let this be angle  two and let this be angle three angles  one two and three are the interior  angles of the triangle and the sum  equals 180 degrees there are two more  angles that are formed outside the  triangle let's call them angle for an  angle five before we move forward let us  understand a new concept assume these  are two parallel lines and there is a  transversal that cuts those lines  let this be angle four and this be angle  two in such a case angle two will always  equal angle 4 these are alternate  interior angles  remember the transversal has to cut two  parallel lines now let's zoom out and  look at the original figure BC and EF  are parallel lines and angle 2 and angle  4 are alternate interior angles which is  why angle 2 will equal angle 4 if you  look at the figure carefully you will  notice that angle 3 and angle 5 are  alternate interior angles to AC is the  transversal which intersects these two  parallel lines it means that angle 3  will also be equal to angle 5 both these  pairs are alternate interior angles what  we are interested in is the sum of these  three angles angle 1 plus angle 2 plus  angle 3 since angle 2 is equal to angle  4 and angle 3 is equal to angle 5  we can write this as angle one plus  angle four plus angle five we just  replaced angles two and three with  angles four and five what does this tell  you look at the figure and see where  angles one four and five are placed EF  is a straight line and since EF is a  straight line we know that the sum of  angle 1 angle 4 and angle 5 will equal  180 degrees it implies that the sum of  angles one two and three will 